/*!
    \page creator-how-to-view-android-logcat.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-android

    \title View Android Logcat output

    Debug Android applications by viewing the log of the device or emulator
    that they run on. The log contains the messages of your application,
    Android services, and the system. If the application crashes, the log also
    contains the stack trace.

    Your application logs messages with \c qDebug() and the other
    \l {<QtLogging>}{Qt logging functions}. By default, Qt uses the name of the
    logging category as the tag of a message, or \c default if the message has
    no category.

    \image {qtcreator-android-logcat.webp} {Logcat tab in Application Output}

    \section1 View Logcat output

    To view the log of a device:

    \list 1
        \li Connect the device and \l{Enable debugging on devices}
            {enable USB debugging on it}, or \l {Start AVDs}{start an
            emulator}.
        \li Go to \uicontrol Tools > \uicontrol Android > \uicontrol Logcat,
            and select the device.
    \endlist

    The log opens in a tab in \l {Application Output}. Each device has its own
    tab. To see the SDK level and serial number of the device, hover over the
    tab.

    When you run an application on a device, \QC opens the tab for you and
    enters \c package:mine in the filter field to show only the messages of
    that application.

    \section1 Read the log

    By default, each line shows the level, tag, and process ID, followed by the
    message:

    \code [text]
    D/default ( 4521): Brewing espresso
    \endcode

    In the \uicontrol Standard view mode, the same line shows the date and
    time, tag, package name, and level in columns:

    \code [text]
    2026-10-06 12:32:41.512 default   io.qt.coffeemachine   D  Brewing espresso
    \endcode

    \QC reads the log while the tab is visible or while an application that you
    started from \QC is running. Between the log messages, \QC adds status
    messages framed by asterisks:

    \code [text]
    **** Phone_API_36 (emulator-5554) - disconnected ****
    \endcode

    Status messages report when the device disconnects or reconnects, when a
    new run stops an earlier one, and when output was skipped while the tab was
    hidden. The filter does not hide them.

    \section1 Change the layout

    To select what each line shows, go to \preferences >
    \uicontrol {Build & Run} > \uicontrol {Application Output}, and set
    \uicontrol {View mode} in the \uicontrol {Android Logcat} group. For
    \uicontrol Standard, select the columns to show. The settings apply to all
    \uicontrol Logcat tabs.

    \image {qtcreator-preferences-application-output.webp}
           {Application Output tab in Build & Run preferences}

    \section1 Filter messages

    To show only the messages that you are interested in, enter a query in the
    filter field of the \uicontrol {Application Output} view. Each tab has its
    own filter.

    A query consists of terms separated by spaces. A message is shown when it
    matches all the terms. The matching is not case sensitive. A term is either
    plain text or one of the keys from the following table with a value.

    \table
    \header
        \li Term
        \li Shows messages that
    \row
        \li \e text
        \li Contain \e text anywhere in the line, including the tag and the
            package name.
    \row
        \li \c {tag:}\e name
        \li Have a tag that contains \e name.
    \row
        \li \c {package:}\e name
        \li Come from an application that has \e name in its package name.
    \row
        \li \c package:mine
        \li Come from your application, including its other processes. This
            is the application that you last ran on the device from \QC, or
            otherwise the application of the active project.
    \row
        \li \c {level:}\e level
        \li Have the given level or a more severe one. Use \c verbose,
            \c debug, \c info, \c warning, \c error, or \c fatal. You can
            abbreviate the level, as in \c {level:warn}.
    \endtable

    To leave out messages, add a minus sign in front of a term. For example, to
    see the warnings and errors of your application, except those with the tag
    \c Choreographer, enter:

    \code [text]
    package:mine level:warning -tag:Choreographer
    \endcode

    \sa {Android}{How to: Develop for Android}, {Developing for Android},
    {Application Output}, {Debug on Android devices}
*/
